{"step_index":315,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-11T02:53:45Z","input_tokens":5314,"cache_read_tokens":52936,"output_tokens":612,"content":"The **Teach Me This Code** visualizer is now fully responsive and optimized for **mobile phones (`<= 768px` & `<= 480px`)** and **tablets (`<= 1180px`)** while keeping the desktop layout untouched.\n\n### What Was Updated for Mobile & Tablet\n1. **Sticky Multi-Row Touch Navigation Bar ([styles.css](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app_build/styles.css#L1511-L1577))**\n   - On mobile screens, the top header automatically wraps into a compact, thumb-friendly layout with a full-width 5-button stepping bar (`← Previous`, `🔍 Sub-Expr`, `Next Step →`, `▶ Auto Play`, `↺ Restart`) so buttons never clip off the screen.\n2. **Scrollable Code Box with Automatic Active-Line Centering ([styles.css](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app_build/styles.css#L1611-L1626), [app.js](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app_build/app.js#L638-L644))**\n   - On phones and tablets, the **Your Code** panel is constrained to a smooth scrollable height (`250px`) and automatically scrolls to keep the highlighted active Python line centered as you tap **Next Step →**.\n   - The horizontal **Iterations & Execution Phases** pill strip also auto-scrolls to keep the active iteration pill in view ([app.js](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app_build/app.js#L747-L753)).\n3. **Mobile-Adapted Teaching, Memory & Iteration Cards ([styles.css](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app_build/styles.css#L1635-L1865))**\n   - **Thonny Sub-Expression Evaluator & Calculations**: Formula rows and long expressions wrap cleanly without horizontal page overflow.\n   - **Stack & Heap Memory Cards**: Variable reference rows (`Name → @Addr → Value`) and Heap object cards wrap cleanly on narrow screens.\n   - **All-Steps & Iterations Explorer**: Iteration cards and step rows automatically switch from a wide 5-column desktop grid to a stacked mobile card layout, and the full dry-run table supports smooth touch scrolling."}
{"step_index":316,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-25T11:34:46Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nImprove my existing Code Flow Visualizer by implementing Phase 1: Detailed Beginner Teaching Steps.\r\n\r\nCRITICAL RULE: Do not rewrite, replace, delete, or refactor my existing code. Do not rebuild the application from scratch. Preserve all existing files, working features, execution logic, and UI design. Only make the smallest necessary additions to support this feature.\r\n\r\nBefore making changes, inspect the existing project structure and identify how the execution engine, step navigation, variable inspector, and explanation panel currently work.\r\n\r\n### Objective\r\n\r\nMake the existing application easier for complete beginners by breaking execution into smaller, understandable teaching steps.\r\n\r\n### Requirements\r\n\r\n1. Preserve the original user-provided Python code exactly. Never modify the code being visualized to make explanations easier.\r\n\r\n2. Preserve the real execution engine and its actual runtime values. Do not fabricate execution steps or variable values.\r\n\r\n3. Add a teaching layer that can display multiple explanation substeps for one actual execution event.\r\n\r\n4. For each supported operation, explain:\r\n   - What is happening?\r\n   - What do the symbols mean?\r\n   - What are the current values?\r\n   - How is the result calculated?\r\n   - Why is this operation necessary?\r\n   - What happens next?\r\n\r\n5. Break compound operations into understandable teaching substeps. For example, for `c += nums[i]`, show:\r\n   - Read the current value of `c`.\r\n   - Read `i`.\r\n   - Look up `nums[i]`.\r\n   - Show the addition using actual values.\r\n   - Show the resulting value.\r\n   - Explain that the result is stored back in `c`.\r\n\r\n6. For a condition such as `if c >= ans:`, show:\r\n   - The current values of `c` and `ans`.\r\n   - The comparison with actual values.\r\n   - The True/False result.\r\n   - The branch that executes.\r\n   - The reason the program moves to the next line.\r\n\r\n7. For loops, explain the current iteration, index, list element, and what causes the next iteration.\r\n\r\n8. For variable assignments, distinguis\n<truncated 293 bytes>\nl execution steps.\r\n\r\n11. Keep Previous Step, Next Step, Restart, and Auto Play functional. Moving backward must restore the correct teaching state and associated runtime snapshot.\r\n\r\n12. If an explanation cannot be derived reliably from actual execution information, display a general explanation rather than guessing.\r\n\r\n### Architecture\r\n\r\nKeep the teaching layer separate from the existing execution engine. It should consume the engine's existing execution events and generate beginner-friendly explanations without changing how the code runs.\r\n\r\nDo not duplicate or hardcode the execution engine.\r\n\r\nDo not introduce AI APIs, API keys, cloud services, or paid dependencies.\r\n\r\n### Testing\r\n\r\nTest the existing maximum-subarray example and verify:\r\n- Every teaching substep appears in the correct order.\r\n- Variable values match the real execution.\r\n- Previous Step and Next Step work correctly.\r\n- The final output remains 6 for the existing example input.\r\n- Existing functionality has not regressed.\r\n\r\n### Delivery\r\n\r\nFirst provide a short summary of the files you intend to change and why. Then implement Phase 1 using minimal changes.\r\n\r\nAfter implementation, report exactly which files changed, what was added, which tests passed, and any remaining limitations.\r\n\r\nDo not begin Phase 2 or modify unrelated features. Preserve the existing application.\n\nPhase 1 — Teach one tiny step at a time\nStart here\nAdd more detailed teaching steps without changing the actual Python code.\n- Explain what a variable is before explaining its assignment.\n- Explain operators such as +=, >=, and < separately.\n- Break each loop iteration into smaller steps.\n- Explain why a line runs and what happens next.\n- Add a simple \"Why am I learning this?\" explanation when useful.\nExample: Instead of jumping from c += nums[i] to the next condition, explain the current number, the calculation, the new value of c, and then the next condition.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-10-25T17:04:46+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>","truncated_fields":["content"]}
